  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 349 PM EDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nAs the cold front departs off to the south and east, surface \nridging will fill in over the Ohio Valley, setting up another \nstretch of dry weather across the region. A few isolated showers and \nstorms are possible (< 20% chance) in far southern Kentucky Friday \nafternoon, but for most of the area conditions will be dry. Looking \nbroadly at the mid and upper-level pattern, the upper trough that \nhas lingered over the eastern CONUS will slowly push off to the \neast, while the upper trough over the western CONUS flattens out \nsome and spreads towards our region. This pattern change will take \nplace through the rest of the long term forecast period, with our \nregion remaining under a flattened ridging setup through Tuesday \nafternoon.\n\nThe main thing to look out for during this period will the the slow \nrise in temperatures, with a culminating return to conditions in the \n90s. Along with this pattern shift will be persistent southerly \nflow at the lower levels, allowing warmer air to fill back in over \nthe region. Per the latest LREF, chances for temperatures at or \nabove 90 will increase each passing day, with less than 25% \nprobabilities Friday afternoon climbing to above 70% Sunday \nafternoon and peaking with greater than 75% chances for Monday. \nTuesday still has elevated chances (> 65%), so there is increasing \nlikelihood in a stretch of hotter conditions. One positive is that \nfairly dry air will remain in place until the latter portions of \nthe forecast period, keeping dew points in the 60s. This will limit \nheat indices some, with LREF outputting near-zero chances of values \nat or above 100 through Monday, and less than 35% chances on Tuesday.",
